# Next.js App Router conventions

Cheap-protection rules that prevent demo-breaking P0s. Production-grade
patterns (DAL, structured logging, `optimizePackageImports`) are deferred
to `TODO.md` — useful but over-engineering for a 4-hour event.

## Secrets — never `NEXT_PUBLIC_`

The `NEXT_PUBLIC_` prefix inlines the value into client-side JavaScript,
visible in any browser DevTools.

```bash
# ✅ server-only
JWT_SECRET=...

# ❌ leaks into the client bundle
NEXT_PUBLIC_JWT_SECRET=...
```

## Server Actions — re-verify auth inside the function

Middleware is for routing, not security (CVE-2025-29927, CVSS 9.1).
Every mutating Server Action verifies the session in its own body.

```ts
'use server'
export async function updateResource(data: unknown) {
  const parsed = Schema.safeParse(data);
  if (!parsed.success) return { error: 'Invalid input' };
  const session = await verifySession();
  if (!session) return { error: 'Unauthorized' };
  // … now safe to mutate
}
```

## Parallel data fetching

Independent fetches use `Promise.all()`. Sequential `await` is 2–10×
slower for no reason.

```ts
// ✅ one round trip
const [user, posts, config] = await Promise.all([
  getUser(), getPosts(), getConfig(),
]);

// ❌ three round trips
const user = await getUser();
const posts = await getPosts();
```

In RSC: split fetches into sibling components and wrap async children
in `<Suspense>` so the layout streams.

## No module-level mutable state in RSC

RSC renders concurrently. Module-level `let` variables race across
requests and can leak one user's data into another's response.

```ts
// ✅ per-request via React.cache()
import { cache } from 'react';
export const getUser = cache(async () => auth());

// ❌ Request B overwrites Request A
let currentUser: User | null = null;
```

## Server components by default

Default to server components. Add `'use client'` only when the file
needs `useState`/`useEffect`/`useReducer`, event handlers, or
`window`/`document`. Push `'use client'` to the leaf and pass
server-fetched data down as props.
